WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.719
Please welcome Matthew Dicks to the stage.

00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:12.209
Thank you.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.530
I'm standing on the

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.920
playground behind my school.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.220
It's recess duty.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.259
When my son was very little, he

00:00:21.259 --> 00:00:23.980
asked me, Is recess duty like duty?

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:25.169
Like poop?

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:26.890
And I said, yeah, actually,

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.199
it is just like that.

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:29.749
And I sent him away.

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:31.170
I am sure that conversation

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.640
created enormous problems for

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.980
him at some point in his life.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.640
But it was an accurate

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.280
description of recess duty.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.320
I waste my precious time putting

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.950
band aids on cuts that are not cuts,

00:00:42.349 --> 00:00:44.400
pretending to listen to stories about

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.150
cousins birthday parties, and telling

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:48.719
mean children that they have no one

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:50.510
to play with because they are mean.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.299
And so I'm standing there doing all

00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:55.029
of these things when I hear these four

00:00:55.029 --> 00:00:56.979
words that strike fear into my heart.

00:00:57.585 --> 00:00:59.295
Never have I ever.

00:01:00.145 --> 00:01:02.165
And I look over and I see three of

00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:03.945
my students, ten year old girls,

00:01:03.965 --> 00:01:05.975
on the sidewalk, and apparently

00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:07.095
they are playing this game.

00:01:07.095 --> 00:01:09.045
So now I am running, because I

00:01:09.055 --> 00:01:10.504
have played Never Have I Ever.

00:01:10.504 --> 00:01:12.695
I understand the dynamics of this game.

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:15.205
This is not a benevolent game where we

00:01:15.205 --> 00:01:17.379
acquire knowledge about our friends.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.670
This is social combat.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.550
This is, we extract information from

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.740
foolish children and then we sharpen

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.890
it and we stab them with it later on.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.530
So I'm running over, like, 1985,

00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:32.260
Jimbo Power's birthday party.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.950
Melissa says, Matt, never

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.310
have you ever kissed someone?

00:01:35.339 --> 00:01:36.509
And immediately I have to

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:38.305
figure out Do I tell the truth?

00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:40.045
Basically what I'm wondering

00:01:40.045 --> 00:01:41.365
is, is everyone else in

00:01:41.365 --> 00:01:42.395
this room kissed somebody?

00:01:42.395 --> 00:01:44.185
Because if so, now I have to lie and

00:01:44.185 --> 00:01:46.345
say yes, and I have to prepare myself

00:01:46.355 --> 00:01:47.805
for the questions that will come, which

00:01:47.805 --> 00:01:49.904
will undoubtedly be, Who did you kiss?

00:01:49.904 --> 00:01:51.625
And since no one has ever kissed me in

00:01:51.625 --> 00:01:53.464
my school, and probably never will, I

00:01:53.465 --> 00:01:55.755
have to make up a story about the time my

00:01:55.755 --> 00:01:57.885
parents took me to the far flung land of

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.510
Pawtucket, Rhode Island or Woburn and I

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.120
was in a candy store and I had a tryst by

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.230
a licorice stand with a girl Story that

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.259
did not work a kid once asked me never

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.309
have you ever gone to third base and I

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:15.209
knew It wasn't about baseball, but that

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:18.720
is all I knew about that question So I am

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.850
running to these girls and as I get close

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.320
I hear one of them say Never have I ever

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.190
gone to bed without brushing my teeth.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:28.970
And I sort of grind to a halt.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.649
Because this is not 1985 Sex

00:02:31.649 --> 00:02:32.980
and Drugs, Never Have I Ever.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.939
This is like Paw Patrol,

00:02:34.939 --> 00:02:35.890
Never Have I Ever.

00:02:35.899 --> 00:02:36.619
It's fine.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:38.729
There's nothing wrong here.

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:41.060
And as I turn to walk away,

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.230
the girls say, Mr. Dicks, you

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.560
want to play Never Have I Ever?

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.240
And they explain to me That

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.440
their game's a competition.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.099
They stand on the sidewalk and if

00:02:48.099 --> 00:02:49.450
you've never ever done something,

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.019
you take a step forward and whoever

00:02:51.019 --> 00:02:52.430
gets to the bench first wins.

00:02:52.649 --> 00:02:53.669
And now I'm all in because

00:02:53.669 --> 00:02:54.809
I love competition.

00:02:55.019 --> 00:02:58.419
I love to crush small children in games

00:02:58.419 --> 00:03:00.309
that are utterly meaningless so I can

00:03:00.309 --> 00:03:02.559
feel better about myself as a human being.

00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:04.809
So I'm standing there on the line with

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:06.549
the girls and the first girl says,

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.690
Never have I ever climbed a mountain.

00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:11.070
And all three of them step forward.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.120
I'm like, are you kidding me?

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.649
You're ten and you've

00:03:13.649 --> 00:03:14.950
never gone up a mountain?

00:03:15.089 --> 00:03:17.009
Like, no lie, when I'm twelve I

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.829
get lost in the white mountains

00:03:18.829 --> 00:03:20.429
of New Hampshire for two days.

00:03:20.689 --> 00:03:22.950
And nobody cares, including me.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.969
They've never climbed a mountain before?

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.849
The next girl says, Never have I ever

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:29.839
gone to bed without a night light on.

00:03:29.839 --> 00:03:30.969
And I think, Oh my god,

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.110
lady, don't say that.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:32.769
Cause we're all gonna

00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:33.690
make fun of you for it.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:36.680
Oh my God, I'm the only one who's

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:37.850
going to make fun of her for this.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.290
Like, this group of children

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.380
that I'm taking care of,

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.049
they're so different than I was.

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:44.990
Like, they've taken all their

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.299
daggers and put them away.

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:48.129
They're sweet, they're accepting.

00:03:48.129 --> 00:03:48.899
It's not all good.

00:03:48.899 --> 00:03:50.219
No, no, it's not all good.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:52.429
Like, they, this generation of children

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.759
can't get ten feet from a water bottle

00:03:54.759 --> 00:03:56.289
without being triggered in some way.

00:03:56.349 --> 00:03:58.160
They're the most hydrated group of

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.399
kids I've ever met in my entire life.

00:04:01.579 --> 00:04:02.719
It is a nightmare.

00:04:03.235 --> 00:04:04.745
They can't go through my math

00:04:04.745 --> 00:04:06.015
lessons without a squishy

00:04:06.015 --> 00:04:07.535
thing or a stuffed animal.

00:04:07.704 --> 00:04:10.084
I had a girl last week in my math lesson

00:04:10.095 --> 00:04:11.725
with a blanket wrapped around her.

00:04:11.905 --> 00:04:13.374
I said, what's up with the blanket?

00:04:13.375 --> 00:04:15.514
She said, it makes me happy during math.

00:04:15.915 --> 00:04:17.475
I said, math isn't happy.

00:04:17.625 --> 00:04:18.474
It's awful.

00:04:18.474 --> 00:04:19.254
It's hard.

00:04:19.254 --> 00:04:20.225
It's onerous.

00:04:20.545 --> 00:04:21.704
I would have loved to have a water

00:04:21.704 --> 00:04:24.275
bottle and a, and a blanket and a stuffed

00:04:24.275 --> 00:04:26.015
animal during math when I was a kid.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:28.470
All I had was angst and despair.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:29.840
It made me who I am.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.170
I can't stand these kids.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.499
And I'm losing the game because I have

00:04:35.499 --> 00:04:37.459
done everything and they've done nothing,

00:04:37.459 --> 00:04:39.360
so they're nine steps ahead of me.

00:04:40.749 --> 00:04:43.730
And then the tallest girl says, Never

00:04:43.739 --> 00:04:47.010
have I ever met my father's expectations.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.730
And I think, that is nonsense,

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:50.860
because I know your fathers.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:52.010
They're all fine men.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:53.270
What are you talking about?

00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:55.205
And one of the girls says, Well, I'm a

00:04:55.205 --> 00:04:57.415
swimmer, and when I get a PR in the pool

00:04:57.415 --> 00:04:59.235
and I come out, my dad's happy for me.

00:04:59.505 --> 00:05:01.784
But then he says, But just imagine how

00:05:01.785 --> 00:05:03.025
much better you could have been if you

00:05:03.025 --> 00:05:05.274
had nailed the kick turn on the third lap.

00:05:05.885 --> 00:05:07.594
And the next girl says, Well, I mastered

00:05:07.594 --> 00:05:09.255
my multiplication facts last month, and

00:05:09.255 --> 00:05:10.735
when I came home to my dad, he said,

00:05:10.735 --> 00:05:12.325
That's great, but why didn't you do it

00:05:12.325 --> 00:05:14.145
in October when you were supposed to?

00:05:14.735 --> 00:05:16.745
And the third one says, My dad always

00:05:16.765 --> 00:05:18.635
tells me how I can be better, and

00:05:18.635 --> 00:05:20.335
I know he does it because he loves

00:05:20.335 --> 00:05:23.105
me, but it never feels like love.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.130
And now I'm panicking.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:26.190
I'm thinking what about my

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:27.460
children, Charlie and Clara?

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:28.770
Have I ever told them that

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:30.430
they've met my expectations?

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:31.350
They never have.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.589
I mean, they don't even come close.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.659
Like, but how have I at least

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.640
fooled them into believing that they

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.880
occasionally meet my expectations?

00:05:40.819 --> 00:05:42.470
And so as I'm panicking about

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.100
this situation, the tall girl

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.310
says, Mr. Dicks, have you ever

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.090
met your father's expectations?

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.440
And I catch for a minute.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:51.570
I don't know what to say.

00:05:52.375 --> 00:05:53.935
But then I know exactly what to say

00:05:53.995 --> 00:05:55.295
because I've been working with kids for

00:05:55.295 --> 00:05:57.664
25 years as a teacher and I know they're,

00:05:58.425 --> 00:06:00.565
they're more capable than we all know.

00:06:01.094 --> 00:06:04.585
And so I tell her, I say, my father left

00:06:04.585 --> 00:06:07.094
when I was seven and he never came back.

00:06:07.474 --> 00:06:09.174
And so I don't think he ever

00:06:09.174 --> 00:06:10.914
had expectations for me.

00:06:11.735 --> 00:06:14.155
And three hands reach out and they

00:06:14.164 --> 00:06:16.765
grab my wrist and they pull me forward.

00:06:17.565 --> 00:06:18.865
And that girl says,

00:06:19.065 --> 00:06:20.715
That counts, Mr. Dicks.

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:25.925
And then she tells me, I'm sorry your

00:06:25.925 --> 00:06:28.275
father abandoned you when you were a kid.

00:06:29.515 --> 00:06:34.094
And it is crazy, but from the age

00:06:34.094 --> 00:06:39.535
of 7 to 52, I have basically been

00:06:39.535 --> 00:06:41.875
waiting for my father to come back.

00:06:42.675 --> 00:06:44.064
And it is not until I'm on a

00:06:44.065 --> 00:06:46.825
sidewalk with a 10 year old girl.

00:06:47.605 --> 00:06:49.145
That I understand for the first

00:06:49.155 --> 00:06:52.395
time that he is not coming back.

00:06:52.855 --> 00:06:55.115
It is the first time I have ever

00:06:55.165 --> 00:06:58.384
in my life heard the word abandoned

00:06:58.415 --> 00:07:01.115
used to describe what happened to me.

00:07:02.105 --> 00:07:04.504
And it is the first time ever that

00:07:04.504 --> 00:07:06.925
a human being has ever said to me,

00:07:08.055 --> 00:07:10.045
I'm sorry that happened to you.

00:07:10.565 --> 00:07:13.335
My friends have said, that sucked.

00:07:13.725 --> 00:07:14.645
It was terrible.

00:07:14.655 --> 00:07:16.054
Well, I know that my wife.

00:07:16.815 --> 00:07:19.725
hates what happened to me as a boy.

00:07:21.205 --> 00:07:23.615
But that ten year old girl, she was the

00:07:23.615 --> 00:07:28.044
first person to tell me, I am sorry for

00:07:28.044 --> 00:07:29.955
what happened to you when you were seven.

00:07:32.945 --> 00:07:36.034
When people ask me today why I am still

00:07:36.444 --> 00:07:39.375
a public school teacher after 25 years,

00:07:39.775 --> 00:07:43.135
with all of the unspeakable, ridiculous,

00:07:43.554 --> 00:07:47.015
nonsensical, impossible things.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.870
that schoolteachers go through today,

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.500
and with, quite honestly, all the other

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:55.030
opportunities I have to leave a classroom

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:57.929
and go do other things with my life, my

00:07:57.929 --> 00:08:01.150
answer is always immediate and simple.

00:08:02.759 --> 00:08:06.250
I love kids, but far more important

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:12.540
than that, kids understand me, and they

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:16.409
speak truth to me, and they love me.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:17.840
Thank you.

00:08:26.719 --> 00:08:27.900
Matthew Dicks!

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.179
Matthew Dicks!

